{"id": "live-396", "title": "Atwood v. Lamar Obie Co., LLC (Portland Wall-Sign Lease, Good Faith and Fair Dealing)", "category": "lease-disputes", "status": "appeal", "date": "2026-11-12", "jurisdiction": "Court of Appeals of the State of Oregon", "state": "OR", "amount": "$1,237,191.92 jury verdict, affirmed on appeal", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/11060193/atwood-v-lamar-obie-co-llc/", "summary": "The Oregon Court of Appeals affirmed, on November 12, 2026, a $1,237,191.92 jury verdict for Portland building owner Jim A. Atwood against billboard company Lamar Obie Company, LLC for breach of the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing. The dispute arose after an ODOT advertising permit tied to Atwood's leased exterior wall sign ended up solely in the name of Lamar's predecessor and then Lamar itself, and Lamar surrendered the permit to the state for a relocation credit it could not use rather than transferring it to Atwood when the lease ended.", "significance": "Shows that a tenant or its assignee can face substantial liability under the implied covenant of good faith and fair dealing for how it handles a government permit tied to a leased commercial use, even without an express lease clause addressing permit ownership, and underscores the exposure a leasehold buyer inherits from a predecessor's permit-naming history.", "judge": "Darleen Ortega", "tags": ["lease-disputes", "billboard", "oregon", "good-faith-and-fair-dealing", "permit-transfer"]}
